format and uc004-testutils—while the others are implemented. Following the priority rule, I should tackle release 00.0 first, but I need to verify the spec status for those two use cases.\n\nI'm noticing something odd: both uc002-format and uc004-testutils show \"not started\" for code status, yet they're not appearing in the requirement_states section at all. This could mean either all their requirements are already complete, or they were never tracked in the first place. The constraint is clear though—I should only propose tasks for items with \"ready\" status in requirement_states, and since these two SRDs aren't listed there, I need to figure out what that actually means for my next steps.\n\nLooking at what IS in requirement_states with ready status, I can see the earliest requirements are spread across srd044-uname, srd045-arch, srd046-nproc, srd047-hostname, and srd048-hostid from release 03.1, all with various ready items I could work with.\n\nBut I'm noticing something odd—I don't see srd005-cat or several other early SRDs in the requirement_states at all, even though they should be there. The list seems to jump from srd004-ts directly to srd008-ls and then continues from srd020 onward, with gaps where srd006, srd007, and srd009 should be.\n\nFor release 01.1 (cat), the code_status shows \"not started\" but there's no corresponding SRD with ready requirements tracked, which means I can't propose tasks for it given the constraint to only work with ready items. Looking at what's actually available, the earliest releases with ready requirements are in batch 3.1—I'm seeing srd044-uname with 16 ready requirements, and several others like srd045-arch, srd046-nproc, srd047-hostname, and srd048-hostid each with 3 to 6 ready requirements.\n\nRelease 03.1 should be the priority, with uname being the most substantial work. But I need to verify the requirement weights against the 4-point limit per task, and I'm realizing I don't have access to the actual weights from requirements.yaml without using tools.\n\nGiven the project convention that most requirements default to weight 1, I'll work with that assumption and cap each task at 4 sub-requirements maximum. Let me map out the ready requirements for release 03.1, starting with uname which has the most components ready.\n\nLooking at the remaining SRDs, arch, nproc, hostname, and hostid all have their core implementations done—the outstanding work is mostly error handling and differential testing in the R3 layer. With only 4 task slots available, I need to prioritize which of these to tackle first. I'm mapping out how to distribute uname's requirements across the 4 available tasks. The R-groups likely represent different aspects—individual flags, combined outputs, and interactions—so I need to figure out which requirements fit together logically rather than just splitting them evenly. Let me reconsider whether grouping by functionality (like all flag implementations together) makes more sense than arbitrary splits. I'm hitting the 4-task limit, so I need to decide whether to focus entirely on uname or split across multiple utilities. Given the constraint, I'll stick with uname since I have clear requirements mapped out, but I'm realizing this means leaving the other utilities unaddressed in this planning phase. I see that each of these utilities has its own package and can't be combined. The constraint is clear—focus on the earliest incomplete release, which is 03.1, and they're all in that same release. Given the 4-requirement limit per task with a total weight of 4, I need to figure out how to allocate these efficiently. Since uname is the largest piece with 16 requirements, I should prioritize getting as much of that done as possible within the task constraints.\n\nBut I'm realizing there's a complication: uname is a new package that hasn't been started yet, which typically means I'd need to propose a contract task first to define the exported types and interfaces. However, since uname is a cmd/ utility with just a main package, that contract requirement might not actually apply here the way it would for a pkg/ package.\n\nLooking at the existing contracts in the codebase, they're all for pkg/ packages like testutils, sys, and format—none of the cmd/ packages have contracts. So I think I can skip the contract step for cmd/uname and move straight to implementation tasks.\n\nAs for the other utilities like arch, hostname, hostid, and nproc, those implementations already exist from earlier tasks, so the R3.x requirements are probably just differential tests that need to be added.\n\nGiven the 4-task limit, I should focus on the core implementation for cmd/uname first, then tackle the remaining test coverage for the other utilities.\n\nFor uname specifically, I'm breaking it down into four tasks: the initial flag implementations, additional flags, combined output handling with error cases, and finally the differential tests. Assuming each requirement has equal weight, this distributes the work evenly across the four tasks at 4 points each.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering how the R-groups map to actual functionality. The roadmap describes uname's core behavior—printing system information through individual flags or all at once with -a. Looking at the GNU uname flags available, I'm thinking the requirements probably organize them as individual flag implementations in the early R-groups, then combined output and error handling in the middle groups, with testing at the end. But I need to be more precise about which flags fall into which R-group to properly weight the tasks.\n\nGiven that there are roughly 9 individual flags to implement, I can't fit them all into a single task with the 4-requirement limit. I'm breaking this into multiple tasks: the first task covers the core flags like default behavior, -s, -n, and -r; the second task handles -v, -m, -p, and whatever comes next in the sequence. R4.3 covers the differential test edge cases, completing that task. Now I'm mapping out the dependency chain: Task 0 establishes the core uname implementation, Task 1 builds on it with additional flags, Task 2 adds the combined output and -a flag functionality, and Task 3 handles all the error cases and testing. I need to reconsider how to split this between implementation and tests—the constraint requires keeping those separate, so the implementation tasks should come first, then the test tasks afterward.\n\nI'm also thinking about whether I need to reference the SRD file directly. I know it's at `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd044-uname.yaml` from the naming pattern, and I can include that in the required_reading without actually reading it. Let me look at how the R-groups are structured in the completed utilities to understand what each section should cover—R1 seems to be core functionality, R2 might be additional features or error handling, and R3 appears to be the test coverage. Looking at the task naming pattern from the git log, I can see how to structure my own tasks. I'll organize them following the same format: the command name, a brief description of what each requirement range covers, and the corresponding srd number with requirement ranges. Let me draft the first task focusing on the core flag implementations for uname. Looking at the scope here, I'm realizing that with 16 total requirements spread across 4 tasks, I should aim for roughly 4 requirements per task to keep them balanced. The earlier utilities like nproc and hostname were simpler and completed in single tasks, but uname has more complexity with its 9 flags plus error handling and signal management. Let me structure the tasks to group related functionality together, starting with the core flags, then moving into combined operations and edge cases. For the file organization, I'll follow the project pattern with cmd/uname/main.go as the entry point.\n\nNow I'm checking whether a contract task is needed here. Since cmd/uname is an executable package with no exported types or interfaces—just a main function—the contract requirement doesn't apply the same way it does for pkg/ packages. The constraint specifically mentions contracts for packages with exports, and cmd packages are just entry points. I'm going back and forth on whether a contract task even makes sense for a main package—cmd/uname has no exported types or interfaces to stub out, so there's nothing meaningful to put in a contract. The constraint seems designed for library packages in pkg/, not command packages. I'll move forward without one and start writing the actual tasks.\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a potential complication with the syscall.Uname implementation, especially on macOS where behavior might differ from Linux. I need to check whether pkg/sys should be wrapping this or if cmd/uname can call it directly.\n\nLooking at the component dependencies, srd044-uname doesn't list any dependencies on pkg/sys or pkg/format, which suggests the command uses the standard library directly. I'll proceed with that assumption and start drafting the tasks based on the SRD requirements.\n\nThe uname requirements break down into four groups: individual flags like -s, -n, -r, -v, -m, -p, -i, -o and default behavior; combined output with -a and field ordering; error handling and SIGPIPE; and differential tests along with --help and --version. I'm starting with the first task covering core flag parsing and system fields, implementing basic flag infrastructure, default kernel name output, the -s, -n, and -r flags, and a SIGPIPE handler.\n\nThe second task adds the remaining platform-specific flags: -v, -m, -p, and -i. The third task brings in the -o flag, the -a combined output flag with proper field ordering, and error handling for invalid flags. Finally, the fourth task handles --version and --help along with comprehensive differential tests covering all flags and their combinations. I'm keeping implementation separate from tests as required, with the first