r handling --version and --help flags, setting up the test infrastructure with BuildBinary and LookPath, and then creating test cases for individual flags, the -a flag, and various flag combinations. I'm realizing Task 3 violates the constraint about keeping implementation and tests separate—it includes both --version/--help modifications to main.go and differential tests in uname_test.go. I need to reorganize so each task modifies only one file. The --version/--help feature should move into its own task, leaving the differential tests as a separate one. I'm trying a different split: Tasks 0-2 each handle 4 implementation requirements (R1.1-R1.4, R1.5-R1.8, and R1.9 plus R2.1-R2.2 and R3.1), which leaves R3.2 (the --version/--help flags) mixed with the test requirements in Task 3. The real question is whether I can move --version/--help into Task 2 by dropping something else, or if I need to accept this mixed task.\n\nActually, I realize I'm making assumptions about what R3.2 even is without seeing the SRD. It might not be --version/--help at all—it could be error handling or something completely different. Since I can't verify the actual requirement content, I should structure the tasks around what I know for certain: implementation and tests need to be separate, and each task can hold at most 4 requirements. With only 4 task slots available and the constraint that tests must be isolated, my best option is to either compress Task 2 down to 3 requirements so I can fit R3.2 there, or accept that Task 3 will contain both R3.2 and the test requirements. I need to place R2.2 somewhere, but mixing implementation with tests violates the constraint. Let me reconsider whether I can group these requirements differently to keep implementation and tests cleanly separated across the four tasks. I realize I don't actually need to fit all requirements into these 4 tasks—the constraint just says not to exceed 4 tasks, so I can leave some for a future session. Let me reorganize to maximize coverage: Task 0 handles the basic flags and SIGPIPE, Task 1 covers more flags, Task 2 tackles the OS flag and ordering logic, and Task 3 can pick up R3.2 along with a few more requirements to round things out. I'm hitting the limit with that arrangement—adding R3.2 pushes Task 2 over. Let me try a different split: Tasks 0-2 each handle four implementation requirements, leaving Task 3 as a combined implementation and testing phase that wraps up the remaining work.\n\nThe key insight is that Task 3 can handle both modifying main.go for R3.2 and creating the test file for R4.1-R4.3 in one go. I should be explicit about what each task modifies in my description so the stitch agent knows exactly what to do.\n\nI also need to include weight calculations in my response and make sure I'm only referencing requirements that actually exist in the ready state, then finalize the task breakdown and think through what reference files to include.\n\nFor the uname utility, I should include pkg/sys/sigpipe.go in the required reading since the stitch agent needs to see how SIGPIPE handling works, even though the project guidelines already mention this pattern. I'll also pull in any similar utilities and the relevant pkg/ contracts that uname depends on.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the final output. I'm settling on plain descriptive titles without prefixes, then verifying the weight budget across all tasks to make sure each one stays within the limit.\n\nNow I need to decide the task ordering—the constraint says to follow canonical order by primary file path alphabetically, which means `cmd/nproc` comes before `cmd/uname`. But I'm reconsidering whether nproc's error handling requirements should take priority since it already has partial implementation started. Option B seems like the right call—finishing nproc first since it's already partially done, then moving to uname. Looking at the breakdown though, I notice nproc, arch, hostname, and hostid all have the same pattern: error handling and differential tests that need to be completed. These are relatively small, focused chunks, so I'm trying to figure out the most efficient way to tackle them.\n\nThe constraint allows tasks to touch up to 5-7 files, which means I could potentially combine test tasks across different utilities if they're simple enough. Let me explore whether bundling the arch and hostname tests together would work, or if I need to keep them separate since they're in different directories. I see that each SRD has its own requirement numbering, so I can mix requirements across different SRDs as long as the total stays at 4. That means I could group arch's three requirements with just one hostname requirement to hit exactly 4. But that leaves hostname and hostid with leftover requirements that need their own tasks, which fragments the work. Let me map out what's actually remaining across all four utilities to figure out the most sensible grouping.\n\nLooking at the full picture, uname has the most work ahead with 16 requirements still to tackle, while the others have 3 or 6 each. That's 31 total requirements to distribute across the remaining task slots. With the alphabetical ordering by command name, I'd naturally start with arch, then hostid, hostname, nproc, and finally uname. But I need to account for dependencies—some of these commands need their implementation requirements completed before their test requirements can even be written.\n\nFor nproc specifically, I'd need to handle the implementation work first (R2.1-R2.3) in one task, then the tests (R3.1-R3.3) in another. That's two of my four available tasks right there. The other commands like arch and hostid appear to already have their implementation done based on the requirement states, so they only need their test requirements completed. I could knock out arch tests and hostid tests in the remaining two tasks, or I might be able to combine some of this work more efficiently. 15 total requirements resolved. That leaves hostname (3 reqs) and uname (16 reqs) for the next batch.\n\nActually, I should reconsider the ordering here. Completing the smaller utilities fully gives me 3 done utilities versus starting the large uname work and leaving it partial. For the generation pipeline, finishing utilities completely is more valuable than spreading effort across many partials. Though the constraint does say to order alphabetically by file path, so I need to follow that: arch tests, hostid tests, hostname tests, then nproc tests.\n\nBut nproc has a dependency—I need the implementation done before the tests. So I'm ordering it as: arch tests first, then nproc implementation and tests together, then hostid tests.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the ordering rules. The constraint about implementation before tests applies within the same package, and I should prioritize all implementations before all tests across packages. So nproc's implementation needs to come before arch's tests, which means the order should be: nproc implementation first, then arch tests, then nproc tests, then hostid tests. I'm weighing whether to include hostid or hostname tests as the second task, but hostid comes first alphabetically, so that's the better choice. Let me verify the dependencies—arch and hostid implementations are already complete from earlier requirements, so their tests have no blocking dependencies and can run independently. So the hostid tests would be independent too. Let me map out the final task breakdown: nproc error handling goes first since the differential tests depend on it, then I can run the arch, hostid, and hostname test tasks in parallel since they have no dependencies. Checking the weights—each task stays under the 4-point limit, so this structure works. 4 ✓\n\nAll good. Now let me write the full YAML.\n\nWait, I need to verify whether nproc's R2.1-R2.3 are actually implementation tasks or something else. Looking at the requirement states, they're all marked ready, and since Task 5