ment states, and they are. I should also make sure my task titles anchor to the specific SRD requirement IDs as instructed. For the design decisions, I'll reference the shared protocols from the architecture—like the differential testing pattern and graceful skip pattern—since those are already established in the codebase rather than inventing new ones. Now I'll structure the final output with these constraints in mind. I'm reconsidering the dependency structure here. Since arch and hostid are already implemented from earlier tasks, their test files can be independent. But nproc's R2.x requirements are still pending implementation, so the nproc tests need to wait for that work to complete first.\n\nFor the test tasks themselves, I should ground the design decisions in the architecture's shared protocols—the differential testing pattern, graceful skips, and output comparison—rather than inventing anything new. Now I'm ready to write out the final YAML with these dependencies clarified. I'm realizing the requirements are too generic—just saying \"implement what the SRD says\" won't give the stitch agent enough concrete guidance. But I'm also constrained not to invent design decisions beyond what the SRD specifies, so I need to be careful about how specific I get without overstepping that boundary.\n\nThe solution is to write descriptive requirements that point the agent toward the SRD while giving it enough context to know what to focus on. Since the agent will read the SRD anyway, I can reference specific sections and behaviors without spelling out every detail myself. For nproc, that means describing what the utility should do—print available processing units with optional flags—and then tying each requirement to the corresponding SRD section so the agent knows exactly where to look. I see the issue with my initial approach—those requirement descriptions are too vague and repetitive. Looking at the golden example, I should be more concise and let the SRD reference do the heavy lifting. The pattern of just citing the SRD requirement number with a brief description works better than repeating \"read the SRD\" in each one.\n\nLet me refine this to be cleaner and more direct, keeping the descriptions short while still giving the stitch agent enough context to know what to implement. I need to verify the alphabetical ordering of the test tasks and finalize the dependency structure. Checking the paths: arch comes before hostid, which comes before nproc, so the test task sequence is correct. The nproc implementation has no dependencies, while the nproc tests depend on that implementation being complete. This gives me the final task order: nproc implementation first, then arch tests, hostid tests, and nproc tests. Before hostname alphabetically, so I'll go with hostid and defer hostname to the next batch. Now I'm writing out the final YAML configuration, making sure the formatting is correct for the orchestrator to parse. I need to verify the reference implementation path for the test tasks—checking whether the hostname test file actually exists in the codebase since hostname is marked as implemented.\n\nLooking at what test files are definitely available, I can see several utilities with completed implementations that have corresponding test files, so I'll point the stitch agent to one of those as a reference example instead. I can see that cmd/arch/main.go exists since the code is marked as implemented, but the test file doesn't yet since the requirements are only at \"ready\" status. The same pattern applies to cmd/hostid/main.go and the other commands—the implementations are done but tests need to be written.\n\nFor the nproc task, I'll need the stitch agent to read the existing main.go to understand the current implementation before making modifications. Now I'm deciding whether to include pkg/testutils/difftest.go or the whole pkg/testutils directory in the required reading—looking at what's exported from testutils like DiffTest